WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.161
Two hours later Brody had moved on from practicing for the hoplitodromos race to practicing for the wrestling event again.

2
00:00:08.162 --> 00:00:11.795
Brody hadn't wrestled much, but he wasn't worried.

3
00:00:11.796 --> 00:00:18.621
Today he was practicing against Demeter's champion, a tall, thin youth with a big nose.

4
00:00:18.622 --> 00:00:24.745
"Got you!" Brody cried as he pinned the other athlete for the third time in a row.

5
00:00:24.746 --> 00:00:30.018
Demeter's champion gritted his teeth. "Never mind," he said.

6
00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:35.667
"I may not be the best at wrestling. But I'll beat you at boxing every

7
00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:42.655
time!" Brody let him go and stood up. "Boxing?" he said to Hermes. "Do I have to do

8
00:00:42.656 --> 00:00:47.125
that?" "Of course," Hermes said. "We'll practice that

9
00:00:47.126 --> 00:00:51.355
tomorrow. I've already started setting up some sparring partners for

10
00:00:51.356 --> 00:00:57.457
you." Before Brody could say anything else, a loud buzzing sound filled the

11
00:00:57.458 --> 00:01:00.831
gymnasium. "What's that?" Julia

12
00:01:00.832 --> 00:01:04.425
exclaimed. Markos covered his head. "Look out

13
00:01:04.426 --> 00:01:11.064
above!" Brody glanced up just in time to see a huge black cloud descending on the

14
00:01:11.065 --> 00:01:17.142
gymnasium. When it got closer, he saw that it was actually a swarm of large

15
00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:24.854
insects. "Gross!" Brody covered his head as the first insects hit him. "What are those

16
00:01:24.855 --> 00:01:32.191
things?" "Cicadas." Hermes sounded disgusted. "Apollo! Did you have something to do with

17
00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:40.332
this?" Apollo was standing nearby, grinning as the other gods' champions raced for cover from the rain of singing

18
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:47.441
insects. Meanwhile Apollo's own champion continued to jog around the track, untouched by the

19
00:01:47.442 --> 00:01:53.700
cicadas. "What makes you think I had anything to do with this?" Apollo asked, still

20
00:01:53.701 --> 00:02:00.681
grinning. Cicadas were pelting every inch of Brody's head and body. One even crawled into his

21
00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:10.558
mouth. "Yuck!" he exclaimed, spitting it out and then running to join Julia and Markos, who were huddled beneath the shelter of an

22
00:02:10.559 --> 00:02:24.082
overhang. "Enough, brother!" Artemis stepped forward, frowning. Ignoring the insects raining down on her, she lifted her bow. "Feathered friends, help

23
00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:28.742
us!" A loud, piercing shriek seemed to tear the sky in

24
00:02:28.743 --> 00:02:33.557
two. A moment later something scurried into the

25
00:02:33.558 --> 00:02:38.792
gymnasium. "What's that?" Brody asked, peering through the veil of

26
00:02:38.793 --> 00:02:44.608
cicadas. "It looks like a bird," Markos said. "It's eating the

27
00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:49.872
cicadas!" "It's a guinea fowl," Julia explained. "My neighbors used to raise

28
00:02:49.873 --> 00:02:54.842
them. They're really noisy. And they'll eat

29
00:02:54.843 --> 00:02:59.308
anything." "Look!" Markos pointed. "Here come more of

30
00:02:59.309 --> 00:03:03.397
them!" Dozens of guinea fowl were running or flying into

31
00:03:03.398 --> 00:03:10.151
view. They immediately began gobbling up the cicadas. Some of them let out sharp cries as they

32
00:03:10.152 --> 00:03:18.395
fed. "This is ridiculous!" Ares marched over to Apollo and Artemis, cicadas crunching beneath his

33
00:03:18.396 --> 00:03:22.607
feet. "How can any of us train under these

34
00:03:22.608 --> 00:03:28.432
conditions?" "Sorry." Apollo shrugged, not looking sorry at

35
00:03:28.433 --> 00:03:34.998
all. "I was just testing out a little trick I might try on the mighty

36
00:03:34.999 --> 00:03:40.418
Alcides." "Hmm." Ares suddenly looked much less

37
00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:48.076
angry. "Now that you mention it, I have a few things in mind for Zeus' mighty champion as

38
00:03:48.077 --> 00:03:53.664
well." He raised his spear to his own head just as his red hair burst into

39
00:03:53.665 --> 00:03:59.079
flame. He lit his spear, and then twirled it, shooting fire at the cicadas.

40
00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:04.189
A guinea fowl let out a squawk as a spark just missed its head.

41
00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:11.174
"Maybe we should get out of here." Julia sounded nervous. "Things are getting a little dangerous."

42
00:04:11.175 --> 00:04:24.281
Hermes sighed. "I suppose we won't get anything done until the others have had their fun," he said. "Besides, it's almost dinnertime. Let's go."

43
00:04:24.282 --> 00:04:27.851
They headed back to Markos' house.

44
00:04:27.852 --> 00:04:34.799
"Are the other gods really going to sabotage Alcides, like they said?" Julia asked Hermes.

45
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.735
He looked surprised. "Of course! What a silly question, girl."

46
00:04:39.736 --> 00:04:47.405
Brody frowned. "But the Olympics are supposed to be about who's the best athlete," he said.

47
00:04:47.406 --> 00:04:51.812
"How can you figure that out when you're all trying to cheat all the time?"

48
00:04:51.813 --> 00:04:55.930
He was afraid that Hermes might get mad at his question.

49
00:04:55.931 --> 00:05:06.135
Instead, the god laughed. "Oh, you mortals! You're always getting worked up about things like that."

50
00:05:06.136 --> 00:05:13.886
He patted Brody on the head. "Don't worry about it. It's not as if any of this matters, after all."

51
00:05:13.887 --> 00:05:20.119
"What do you mean?" Julia asked. "I thought the Olympic Games were super important.

52
00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:26.568
Don't the games help to keep peace among all the Greek cities? And honor Zeus and the other gods?"

53
00:05:26.569 --> 00:05:31.002
Hermes pointed to a dog chasing a rat along a path.

54
00:05:31.003 --> 00:05:35.508
"To those creatures, their game is important," he said.

55
00:05:35.509 --> 00:05:41.311
"To you mortals, the Games are important. To us?" He shrugged again.

56
00:05:41.312 --> 00:05:53.000
"The Games are amusing, yes. They bring us pride and a chance to brag, certainly. But important?" He laughed loudly.

57
00:05:53.001 --> 00:05:58.514
Brody was still thinking about what Hermes had said when the family finished dinner that evening.

58
00:05:58.515 --> 00:06:03.593
He waited until he, Julia, and Markos were alone in the courtyard.

59
00:06:03.594 --> 00:06:11.672
"So what did Hermes mean earlier?" he asked Julia and Markos. "Doesn't he care about the Olympics?"

60
00:06:11.673 --> 00:06:22.722
"I told you before," Markos said. "The gods are different from mortals. We are playthings to them—pets, like that dog we saw."

61
00:06:22.723 --> 00:06:29.838
Julia frowned. "I don't even think they see us the way we see dogs or other pets," she said.

62
00:06:29.839 --> 00:06:38.448
"We're more like ants to them. They make us race each other, and then they pull our legs off or stomp on us for fun!"

63
00:06:38.449 --> 00:06:43.167
"You're lucky you're Hermes' champion, Brody," Markos said.

64
00:06:43.168 --> 00:06:46.610
"What are you talking about?" Julia blurted out.

65
00:06:46.611 --> 00:06:49.827
"Hermes might not ever let us ever return home!"

66
00:06:49.828 --> 00:06:54.481
"That's true," Markos admitted with a grim expression.

67
00:06:54.482 --> 00:07:00.443
"But I hate to think of what might happen to Hera's hero if he fails to defeat Alcides.

68
00:07:00.444 --> 00:07:05.138
Or what could happen to Alcides himself if he disappoints Zeus."

69
00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:11.248
"Hmm . . ." Brody hadn't realized that some of the gods could be so cruel.

70
00:07:11.249 --> 00:07:17.157
"Well, it sounds as if Alcides might not even make it to the Olympics," he pointed out.

71
00:07:17.158 --> 00:07:21.871
"I mean, every god out there is going to try to take him down, right?"

72
00:07:21.872 --> 00:07:33.923
"Except Athena and Zeus," Julia said. "Okay, maybe you're right, Markos. Maybe it could be worse."

